<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The hardened runtime's one exception the helper is signed with (spec §5.12, §10): .NET's JIT writes and runs code
  in MAP_JIT memory (allow-jit). The trial also had allow-unsigned-executable-memory and disable-library-validation,
  which a signed build without them showed it doesn't need (2026-10-29): package.sh signs the bundle's native
  libraries with the helper's own identity, so library validation accepts them. No sandbox: the helper starts
  LibreOffice, and keeps its copies in its own folders.

  apple-events lets the hardened runtime send Apple events to other apps: the helper drives Word, Excel and
  PowerPoint with AppleScript (M3.5 spec §5.2, §5.11). Without it macOS refuses the events without asking, and
  Info.plist's NSAppleEventsUsageDescription is what the Automation prompt says. No other app is named: macOS asks
  once for each app it drives.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
